# Laya step 4a: one hardware context for all six bf16 GEMM shapes. Desktop 2, 2026-10-13 UTC,
# mlir-aie ironenv (Python 3.13, IRON v1.4.2 checkout), pyxrt from the XRT SDK. The NPU read idle before every run.
# Tool: tools/gemm_generic_probe.py (sha256 47607bf7f6bc08a198b1365707f66f6f3aff46ae38888b55bf04ab0b729b149b, final).
# Design: kernels/gemm_generic/whole_array_generic.py (sha256 3b341da92b3afaef454daa4df420de50bd2598e9e1e26e10ff7b8f35ed79d199, final).
# Pre-registrations were written in order before each run they cover; each is reproduced with its sha256.

# ===== pre-registration 1 (prereg.txt, sha256 e66648dfcaa52fe2e86410fe20c2e9fbfd8151be88ba52cfc79db4fb734703b7) =====
#   written_utc 2026-10-13T01:01:07Z, Desktop 2, before any NPU run of this design. Laya step 4a.
#   tool: tools/gemm_generic_probe.py (sha256 e5f99dfbd5b2419f02390f220bb3f21a42f8be7894291326779f50349d2dc4b3); design: kernels/gemm_generic/whole_array_generic.py (sha256 bfb581b40947b0929e0c7968b1056923beda2d5c0404a553f4dd66334d48b140); builds: scratch/gemm_generic/aot4 (g_* generic, p_* plain), all m=32 k=64 n=64, C single-buffered, bf16 in, f32 out.
#   Offline result already in hand (not a device claim): the six generic xclbins differ only in bytes that also differ between two builds of the same shape (0x168, 0x19c, the UUID at 0xecc8).
#   Command: python tools/gemm_generic_probe.py run --out scratch/gemm_generic/aot4 --blocks 6 --seqs 5 (30 timed sequences per arm, alternating blocks).
#   G1: every shape, in each arm: max|NPU-numpy|/max|numpy| < 1e-3, padded columns 0, and two runs bit-equal.
#   G2: every shape: the one-context generic output is bit-equal to the six-context plain output on the same inputs.
#   G3: the one-context arm's 120-product sequence IQR lies wholly below the six-context arm's IQR. Kill line: G3 fails -> one context buys nothing beyond noise.
#   Not a gate, stated now: the six-context arm should cost about 1-2 ms more per shape change than the one-context arm (step 3 measured 217.1 ms for Laya's order in IRON). Reported after: laya_npu_screen.py chain --tile best in the same sitting, as continuity with step 3's 556.9 ms; its tiles differ (m=64), so it is context, not a control.
#   Stop rule: any dispatch timeout or non-COMPLETED state stops the run; it is logged and the user is told before any rerun.

# ===== pre-registration 2 (prereg2.txt, sha256 768607bde6412619b608a2a780d2ca6b84d683596a15c64e166bf70e5df0b66f) =====
#   written_utc 2026-10-13T01:09:23Z, after the 4a stop (K=2624 timed out on the generic design, one context) and before any further NPU run. The user approved this confirmation test and the padded rerun.
#   tool sha256 9fd9ef7c6fe33d901b56808e33f51fb1d1e310caa23a6e125113eec9dd40adb7; design sha256 bfb581b40947b0929e0c7968b1056923beda2d5c0404a553f4dd66334d48b140 (unchanged). Builds: scratch/gemm_generic/aot5 (--even-k: K=2624 run as 2688), plus g_1088_1024 (17 k-tiles, odd).
#   Hypothesis H_odd: the generic core hangs when the run-time K-tile count is odd.
#   Confirmation test, in this order, each a fresh process on the generic xclbin g_1024_3072 of aot5: (1) single K=2624 --even-k (42 tiles) -> predicted PASS; (2) single K=1088 (17 tiles, odd) -> predicted TIMEOUT. H_odd survives only if both predictions hold. If (2) leaves the device refusing contexts, wait for xrt-smi idle and a successful context before going on.
#   Then the padded rerun: python tools/gemm_generic_probe.py run --out scratch/gemm_generic/aot5 --even-k --blocks 6 --seqs 5, with gates G1-G3 as in prereg.txt (sha e66648df...), every shape at its run K (2688 for Wo2) in both arms.

# ===== pre-registration 3 (prereg3.txt, sha256 2d6636fe72b8240b3dd50678ee3bb7de4a26fb38029a0ebd47791fbaaf4eadd5) =====
#   written_utc 2026-10-13T01:09:59Z, AFTER the confirmation test's first results and before the runs below.
#   Result so far: H_odd is REFUTED. single K=1088 (17 k-tiles, odd) COMPLETED and matched numpy (8.51e-07), where H_odd predicted a timeout. The K=2688 single did not run: a probe bug (w[:, :N] broadcast with K padded), now fixed to w[:K, :N]; tool sha256 c757c92c4a709e53b928f7da6787877c346ef9fc9e63303948829224dd6299af.
#   Next, each in a fresh process on the generic design: (1) K=2624 --even-k on aot5 (run as 2688); (2) K=2624 unpadded on aot4's generic xclbin, alone. No prediction is made for (2): if it passes alone, the 4a hang depended on what ran before it in the same context; if it times out, it is the shape itself.
#   Then the padded rerun as in prereg2.txt. A timeout in it stops it, as before.

# ===== pre-registration 4 (prereg4.txt, sha256 81bdccbe6458f1db0dd46739eb2850326319fb46c4fec67b3deaea286d5285bc) =====
#   written_utc 2026-10-13T01:16:29Z, after the padded rerun stopped (K=2624-as-2688 timed out after the three K=1024 shapes in one context) and before the bisection. The user approved the bisection.
#   tool sha256 f4e52bd8ec618669e54e25f8dab4687a396b8a1d3b29ada8a99a52cbef1c34a2 (single now takes --seq); builds scratch/gemm_generic/aot4 (unpadded); design g_1024_3072.xclbin; each line a fresh process and context; a timeout ends only that line.
#   Suspect S5248: the N=5248 (run 5376, 21 column tiles per core column, the only odd count) product leaves the context in a state where the next call hangs.
#   B1 [5248, 2624]: S5248 predicts TIMEOUT at 2624. B2 [3072, 1024, 2624]: S5248 predicts PASS throughout. B3 [5248, 1024]: S5248 predicts TIMEOUT at 1024 (any shape after it). B4 [5248, 5248, 2624]: reported, no prediction (does a second 5248 restore parity?).
#   If B1-B3 all hold, the planned fix is N=5248 padded to 6144 (24 column tiles per core column), to be built and tested before any rerun.

# ===== pre-registration 5 (prereg5.txt, sha256 5f146c93f2d42805343aaf1b3e96921561b7d7e69ea6329c0a57bffa82555f44) =====
#   written_utc 2026-10-13T01:20:05Z, after the bisection and before any run of the fixed build.
#   Bisection result: B1/B2/B4 FAILED with wrong values (2624 after any K=1024 call), B3 PASSED: S5248 refuted. H_stale: the core runs each call after the first on the previous call's RTP values, because the barrier wait is an acquire-equal (llvm.aie2.acquire(54, 1)) that nothing consumes; upstream's epoch pattern ends with barrier.release_with_value(1).
#   Fix: release_with_value(1) at the end of core_fn_generic; design sha256 3b341da92b3afaef454daa4df420de50bd2598e9e1e26e10ff7b8f35ed79d199; builds scratch/gemm_generic/aot6 (unpadded K); the core IR now has release(54, 1); the six g_* images still differ only in build-noise bytes.
#   Test, each a fresh process on g_1024_3072.xclbin of aot6: B1 [5248,2624], B2 [3072,1024,2624], B3 [5248,1024], B4 [5248,5248,2624], B5 [2624,1024]. H_stale with the fix predicts PASS on every call. Any FAIL or timeout refutes the fix and stops.
#   If all pass: the full sitting, python tools/gemm_generic_probe.py run --out scratch/gemm_generic/aot6 --blocks 6 --seqs 5, gates G1-G3 as in prereg.txt, unpadded K.

# ===== pre-registration 6 (prereg6.txt, sha256 a34e7626dc98e494b199f6414e080bd537d3853cdb0767bfdd4f1812023b5e9b) =====
#   written_utc 2026-10-13T01:21:21Z, after the full sitting on aot6 stopped and before any timing data exists.
#   What happened: G1 PASSED for the one-context arm on all six shapes (relative error <= 1.94e-06, padded columns 0, repeats bit-equal). The six-context control could not be opened: hw_context creation failed with 0xc01e0009 (this device holds at most five), and the process segfaulted in teardown. No G2 or G3 data exists.
#   Amended control, fixed now: the plain designs with at most 5 contexts resident and least-recently-used eviction, which is what IRON's npu1 runtime does at that ceiling (evict and retry). The probe counts context creations per block. tool sha256 00250dbe11cfe529cb1b70fd0d342f6ed42debe9d518e83b54ca1a309431bd54; design unchanged (sha256 3b341da92b3afaef454daa4df420de50bd2598e9e1e26e10ff7b8f35ed79d199).
#   Gates as before: G1 both arms; G2 one-context output bit-equal to the per-shape plain output on all six shapes; G3 one-context IQR wholly below the per-shape IQR. Command: python tools/gemm_generic_probe.py run --out scratch/gemm_generic/aot6 --blocks 6 --seqs 5.
#   amendment 2026-10-13T01:21:31Z: the first launch stopped before opening any context on a print bug (arm.ctx no longer exists); fixed the print only, tool sha256 47607bf7f6bc08a198b1365707f66f6f3aff46ae38888b55bf04ab0b729b149b.

