# ===== reading =====
# 1. The offline result: with the K-tile and tile counts moved into a run-time parameter, the six shapes compile to
#    device images that differ only in bytes that also differ between two builds of one shape (0x168, 0x19c, and
#    the UUID at 0xecc8 of the AIE partition). One loaded design serves all six through their instruction streams.
# 2. The first two sittings timed out at K=2624 after the K=1024 shapes. The odd-K-tile hypothesis was REFUTED
#    (K=1088, 17 tiles, passed alone), and so was the N=5248 suspect (B2 failed without it). The bisection showed
#    that a K change after the first call in a context gave wrong values (relative error 1.1-1.2).
# 3. Cause, with the alternatives ruled out above: the core waits on the barrier with an acquire-equal
#    (llvm.aie2.acquire(54, 1)), which does not consume it. Nothing in the first design handed it back, so
#    from the second call on, the core read the run-time parameters before the call wrote them, and ran on the
#    previous call's values. A larger stale tile count is harmless, which is why N changes passed.
#    Ending each call with barrier.release_with_value(1), as upstream ml/mm_activation_epilogue does, fixed
#    every failing sequence (5 of 5 pass).
# 4. The control as first registered was infeasible: this device holds at most five of these hardware contexts
#    (the sixth failed with 0xc01e0009, as this repo measured before). The amended control is IRON's own npu1
#    behaviour at that ceiling: at most five resident, least recently used evicted. It made 26 context creations
#    per block of 6 sequences.
# 5. Result, run 4: G1 and G2 pass (all six shapes match numpy within 1.94e-06, and the one-context output is
#    bit-equal to the per-shape output). G3 passes: 120 products in Laya's order take 213.0 ms (median; IQR
#    211.7-214.6) on one context, against 444.5 ms (IQR 429.2-453.2) per shape. The saving is 231.5 ms per
#    sequence, against step 3's 217.1 ms switching estimate.
#    Both arms time the device side only (sync in, call, wait, sync out); conversion is excluded from both.
# 6. Continuity: IRON's chain at the best tiles reads 734.3 ms per sequence (median), of which conversion is
#    175.4 ms. Its device side, sync in + call + sync out = 552.0 ms, against 556.9 ms in step 3 (2026-09-26).
#    The one-context path, raw pyxrt with no IRON wrapper, is 213.0 ms on that same device side. Its tile is m=32
#    against IRON's m=64 for five shapes, and its relative errors equal IRON's at every shape.
# Not measured here: energy; the host side of a sequence (step 3's hostshare); the hybrid end to end.
